      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm working on my thesis regarding heat-induced browning. Can you explain the specific roles that amino acids and reducing sugars play in the Maillard reaction? Also, I need to know specifically how pH levels influence the rate of the reaction.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In the Maillard reaction, amino acids and reducing sugars react to form new flavor compounds, browning, and other volatiles, contributing to the complex developed flavors of cooked foods. Amino acids, particularly those containing basic or neutral side chains, act as nucleophiles, leading to the formation of melanoidins, the brown pigments resulting from the reaction. Reducing sugars, such as glucose and fructose, provide reducing power, enabling the radical-catalyzed sterol cleavage that contributes to the browning process.\n\nThe pH level of the reaction mixture significantly influences the rate of the Maillard reaction. Lower pH (more acidic) conditions increase the reactivity of the Maillard reaction by increasing the availability of protons to facilitate the oxidation of sulfur compounds and the formation of melanoidins.",
      "source": "target",
