    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. a quick follow up then—could you expand on the distinction between the Maillard reaction and caramelization? specifically regarding the temperature thresholds and the types of molecules involved in each.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Maillard reaction and caramelization are both non-enzymatic browning reactions, but they occur under distinct conditions and involve different types of molecules. The Maillard reaction primarily occurs above 140°C (284°F), typically between 140°C to 180°C (284°F to 356°F), at pH ranges around physiological or slightly alkaline (around 6-7), involving protein-bound amino acids and reducing sugars.\n\nCaramelization, on the other hand, occurs at slightly lower temperatures (typically between 120°C to 140°C or 248°F to 284°F) and often takes place over a longer period. Caramelization typically involves a wider range of molecules, including polysaccharides, glycolipids, and other organic compounds, including sugars and phenolic compounds.",
      "source": "target",
